--TEST--
`Core\Cli\Color`'s two constructors refuse a value their colour space has no entry for, on both sides of the bound — `rule:tooling/styling-is-a-value-not-a-grammar`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// A palette has 256 entries and a channel has 256 levels, so both constructors
// have a last accepted value and a first refused one. Both sides are asserted
// together: a constructor that stopped one entry early would print plausibly
// against either half alone.
echo Core\Cli\Text::styled("last", Core\Cli\Style::of({color: Core\Cli\Color::index(255)})), "\n";
try {
    Core\Cli\Color::index(256);
    echo "broken: 256 is not a palette entry", "\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $e) {
    echo $e->message, "\n";
}

echo Core\Cli\Text::styled("last", Core\Cli\Style::of({color: Core\Cli\Color::rgb(255, 255, 255)})), "\n";
try {
    Core\Cli\Color::rgb(256, 0, 0);
    echo "broken: 256 is not a channel level", "\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $e) {
    echo $e->message, "\n";
}

// Each channel is checked, not only the first — a refusal that read one
// argument would pass everything above.
try {
    Core\Cli\Color::rgb(0, 0, 300);
    echo "broken: the blue channel was not checked", "\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $e) {
    echo $e->message, "\n";
}
--EXPECT--
last
Core\Cli\Color::index: 256 is not a palette entry, which is 0 to 255
last
Core\Cli\Color::rgb: `red` is 256, and a channel is 0 to 255
Core\Cli\Color::rgb: `blue` is 300, and a channel is 0 to 255
